ckground_tasks` first, with the transcript as the fallback when the payload carries no list; a turn end with outstanding work is waiting, not held, unless its only outstanding work is non-awaited shells, which a gated run judges as a plain turn end and an ungated run holds once they pass the shell wait bound. `Attach` waits on a held run, and on a legacy `asking` record whose strand is still live, instead of respawning beside it. `died` is narrow: it means a strand reed STILL TRACKS has a pane that is not alive, the provider never came up inside the startup window, or the provider's process is proven gone behind a live pane shell (by the process registry, or a process-ending `SessionEnd` line past the wait's first liveness tick), with `done` instead when every output file exists. Reed's strand table losing the strand entirely — a `reed down`/`remove`, a deleted or rebuilt `reed.json`, a worktree renamed under an in-flight run — is reported as a mechanism failure carrying the run's identity, never as `died`, because reed's bookkeeping going away says nothing about the agent, whose process is often still working in its pane. `PreToolUse` guardrails deny the in-process `Agent` tool in both interactive and autonomous runs (on by default, switchable off via `claude.yaml`'s `deny_agent_tool`, and narrowed in a `ForkSubagents` run to permit fork subagents while still refusing every other subagent type), and `AskUserQuestion` too when the run is autonomous (`Interactive: false`, the default); a `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail denies `python`, `python3` and `python3.<minor>` in command position in every run mode (on by default, switchable off via `shuttle.yaml`'s `deny_python`), and a second `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail, installed in every run mode with no switch, denies a raw module-wide `go test`, `go build` or `go vet`, a raw `go test` over the `tmux` or `llm` tier, and any command naming the gate-slot or strand-name environment variable, steering the session to `lym gate test`, and a third `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail, installed in every run mode with no switch, denies an `rm` on a computed path (a command substitution or a variable expansion in the command) so no permission dialog stalls the run, and a fourth `PreToolUse(Bash)` guardrail, installed in every run mode with no switch, denies `sed -i`, `perl -i`, and a redirect, heredoc or `tee` into a source file, steering the session to read a file with Read and change it with Edit or Write; the python deny's steer carries the same edit rule. The engine announces each deny it installs to the session's system prompt, and every Claude session it launches with a run directory also gets the creel trust sentence and a sentence asking it to request every call whose arguments it already knows in one message, through one `append-system-prompt.txt` file named by `--append-system-prompt-file` on launch and resume, so a session knows the tool is unavailable before it tries one, follows `[creel]` mail from its hooks and batches independent calls. The engine sets the prompt-cache TTL per role on launch and resume from `claude.yaml`'s `prompt_cache_ttl` and `prompt_cache_ttl_roles` (template: 5 minutes for `driver`, 1 hour for `webster` and `orch`, 5 minutes for every other role). `claude.yaml`'s `keep_warm_cap` (template 250s) and `keep_warm_cap_roles` set the per-role keep-warm cap beside them. The provider is swappable behind an **engine** seam; the engine is picked from the registry by `shuttle.yaml`'s `engine:` key, Claude being the one registered engine. Per-run `Model`/`Effort` knobs (`lym shuttle run --model`/`--effort`; effort values `low|medium|high|xhigh|max`, empty = provider default) are engine-validated, not policed by `Spec.validate`. `Spec.Version` is a programmatic engine-validated version pin (claudeengine composes the pinned model id; no CLI flag — consumers drive it via the model-spec notation's `v=` param). Starting a run returns only once its provider is past its startup gates, and a provider that never comes up is torn down (run dir and last pane capture kept) and reported as an error from `Start`, or as `died`/`timeout` from `Run`. ✅ Implemented. See the `internal/shuttleengine` package documentation.
